     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     viewModelScope / lifecycleScope semantics per developer.android.com "Use Kotlin coroutines with lifecycle-aware
     components"; checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A user logs out. The next morning, the server shows that their device kept syncing their old account for six hours. The code review had approved it: <code>SyncEngine</code> was correctly <code>@Singleton</code>. Nobody asked when its coroutines stop, because the word "scope" seemed to answer that already.</p>

Android code uses "scope" for two unrelated things. A **Hilt scope** decides how many instances exist and which container holds them. A **coroutine scope** decides when launched work is cancelled. A `@Singleton` says nothing about when its background work ends. This chapter separates the two, shows where they line up by design, and where you must connect them yourself.

<!-- @after Two Different "Scope" Concepts :: visual -->
### Same word, two questions

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Hilt scope: how many?</span><div class="node"><strong><code>@Singleton</code>, <code>@ViewModelScoped</code>, …</strong><small>one shared instance per component</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Ends when</strong><small>the component is destroyed with its Android lifetime</small></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Says nothing about</strong><small>work an instance has launched</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Coroutine scope: until when?</span><div class="node"><strong><code>viewModelScope</code>, <code>lifecycleScope</code>, your own</strong><small>a <code>Job</code> that parents launched work</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Ends when</strong><small><code>cancel()</code> runs, by you or by a lifecycle</small></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Says nothing about</strong><small>how many objects exist</small></div></div></div><figcaption>"Scoped to the activity" is ambiguous until you say which of the two you mean.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after How They Interact (or Don't) :: animation -->
### One session, both kinds of scope

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">Instances and jobs across a user's session</span><div class="stage viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Hilt instances</span><div class="node" data-el="engine"><strong><code>SyncEngine</code> (<code>@Singleton</code>)</strong><small data-text="engineV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="pricing"><strong><code>PricingState</code> (<code>@ViewModelScoped</code>)</strong><small data-text="pricingV">—</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Coroutine work</span><div class="node" data-el="syncJob"><strong>sync loop</strong><small data-text="syncJobV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="promoJob"><strong><code>applyPromo</code> in <code>viewModelScope</code></strong><small data-text="promoJobV">—</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Log in","caption":"The singleton engine is created on first use and starts its loop in its own scope. Nothing ties that scope to the session.","add":["engine","syncJob"],"state":{"engine":"done","syncJob":"active"},"text":{"engineV":"one instance for the process","syncJobV":"running every minute"}},
{"label":"Open checkout","caption":"The ViewModel's component creates PricingState. A promo request runs in viewModelScope; the singleton repository it calls runs inside that same coroutine.","add":["pricing","promoJob"],"state":{"pricing":"done","promoJob":"active"},"text":{"pricingV":"one per CheckoutViewModel","promoJobV":"request in flight"}},
{"label":"Rotate","caption":"The ViewModel survives, so both its scoped instance and its coroutine scope survive. They line up by design.","state":{"pricing":"done","promoJob":"active"},"text":{"pricingV":"same instance","promoJobV":"still running"}},
{"label":"Leave checkout","caption":"The ViewModel is cleared: PricingState goes with its component, and viewModelScope is cancelled, aborting the in-flight request.","state":{"pricing":"ghost","promoJob":"ghost"},"text":{"pricingV":"released","promoJobV":"cancelled"}},
{"label":"Log out","caption":"The singleton remains, correctly. Its loop also remains, because no lifecycle cancels a scope you created yourself. This is the six-hour sync.","state":{"engine":"done","syncJob":"error"},"text":{"syncJobV":"still running after logout"}},
{"label":"Tie the job to the session","caption":"Give the engine stop(), or a scope created at login and cancelled at logout. The instance stays app-wide; the work now has the session's lifetime.","state":{"syncJob":"ghost"},"text":{"syncJobV":"cancelled on logout"}}
]</script><figcaption>A Hilt scope chooses the container. Only cancellation decides when the work stops.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @diagram 2e121c614e2c3c07 -->
### Shared anchors, separate management

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Hilt components</span><div class="node"><strong><code>SingletonComponent</code></strong><small>from <code>Application.onCreate</code>; holds singletons and their private scopes</small></div><div class="node"><strong><code>ActivityRetainedComponent</code> → <code>ViewModelComponent</code></strong><small><code>@ActivityRetainedScoped</code>, <code>@ViewModelScoped</code></small></div><div class="node"><strong><code>ActivityComponent</code>, <code>FragmentComponent</code></strong><small><code>@ActivityScoped</code>, <code>@FragmentScoped</code></small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Coroutine scopes</span><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong><code>viewModelScope</code></strong><small>cancelled when the ViewModel is cleared</small></div><div class="node"><strong><code>lifecycleScope</code></strong><small>cancelled when the activity or fragment is destroyed</small></div><div class="node"><strong><code>viewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope</code></strong><small>cancelled when the fragment's view is destroyed</small></div></div></div><figcaption>They share lifecycle anchors such as activity start and ViewModel clear, but each is created and ended by its own mechanism.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Pitfalls :: check -->
**Which scope, of which kind?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>Collect a Flow to update a Fragment's views.</summary>

`viewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope` with `repeatOnLifecycle(STARTED)`: work tied to the view, paused while hidden. No Hilt scope is involved.
</details>

<details><summary>Upload pending analytics even if the user leaves the screen.</summary>

Not `viewModelScope`, which would cancel it. Use an application-scoped component with its own bounded scope for quick work, or WorkManager if it must survive process death.
</details>

<details><summary>A per-user cache that must disappear at logout.</summary>

A custom Hilt component for the session, plus a coroutine scope created at login and cancelled at logout, so the instances and their work end together.
</details>
